# Weekly secret audit of $HOME critical paths (dotfiles, ~/bin, ~/repos,
# AI session logs). It masks first, then scans (sd:1254).
#
# The mask pass rewrites key values in session logs, shell history and agent
# scratchpads to <masked:...>, in place. Sessions keep echoing the exported
# keys into new log lines, so a one-time mask silenced one Monday only; the
# operator ruled on 2026-10-07 to mask every week before the scan.
# --no-prune leaves retention to the nightly prune in local-maintenance.
# mask skips a file modified in the last S4S_MASK_SETTLE_MIN minutes (10),
# because a live session may be appending to it, and lists it as busy.
#
# The scan then exits 2 only on a durable finding: a hit outside the mask
# targets, or one mask should have reached. Exit 2 counts as a job failure
# on purpose: it triggers the failure notification (macOS banner + ntfy
# push), so a new leak reaches your phone. A hit in a mask target modified
# inside the window reads "settling", and agent scratchpads and
# ~/.codex/shell_snapshots read "transient": printed, never paged on.
#
# `;`, not `&&`: a failed mask must not skip the scan. The job keeps the
# mask's exit code too: a write that failed part way leaves a freshly
# modified file, whose hits the scan reads as settling, so the scan alone
# would exit 0 over a failed mask. A scan finding (2) wins; else the mask's.
# Output in logs/secret-scan-weekly.log.
JOB_SCHEDULE="0 7 * * 1"
JOB_COMMAND="sh \"$ROOT/../local-scan-for-secrets/scan-for-secrets.sh\" mask --apply --no-prune; mask_rc=\$?; sh \"$ROOT/../local-scan-for-secrets/scan-for-secrets.sh\" critical; scan_rc=\$?; [ \"\$scan_rc\" -ne 0 ] && exit \"\$scan_rc\"; exit \"\$mask_rc\""
